   "profile": "J2",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What a Two-Step Equation Is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps, like 2x + 3 = 11.\n- Goal: get the variable x alone on one side.\n- Undo operations with the opposite operation.\n\n## Steps One and Two\n- Step one: undo the added 3 by subtracting 3 from both sides.\n- Step two: divide both sides by 2, giving x = 4.\n- Layout: 1 undo +3: 2x = 8; 2 undo ×2: x = 4; 3 x alone.\n- Undo addition or subtraction first.\n\n## The Balance Rule\n- Whatever you do to one side, do to the other side.\n- doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced\n- Subtracting 3 from only one side makes the equation untrue.\n\n## Always Check\n- Check by putting your answer back in the original equation.\n- 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11.\n- Both sides match, so the answer is correct.\n\n## Second Example\n- 3x - 5 = 10: add 5 to both sides, giving 3x = 15.\n- Divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5.",
    "mirror": "## What a two-step equation is and what you are aiming for\n- Take 2x + 3 = 11 as the case: it needs two steps to solve, so it is a two-step equation.\n- From that case comes the general goal: get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign.\n- You reach that goal by undoing what was done to x, because you use the opposite operation.\n\n## Step one: undo the addition or subtraction\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the number 3 is added, so step one subtracts 3 from both sides.\n- That subtraction leaves 2x = 8, so the addition is undone and only the multiplication is left.\n- The rule the case shows is that step one is to undo addition or subtraction.\n\n## Step two: undo the multiplication or division\n- In 2x = 8 the x is multiplied by 2, so step two divides both sides by 2.\n- That division gives x = 4, so the variable is now alone on one side.\n- The rule the case shows is that step two is to undo multiplication or division.\n\n## The balance rule: do the same thing to both sides\n- Suppose you subtract 3 from only one side: the equation is no longer true, because the sides no longer match.\n- So the most important rule is that whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale, so doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n- Layout: left side 2x + 3 --subtract 3--> 2x | right side 11 --subtract 3--> 8 | same move on both sides, so it stays balanced.\n\n## Check the answer, then a second example\n- Take the answer x = 4 and put it back: 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11.\n- The left side equals the right side, so x = 4 is correct.\n- So always check your answer by putting it back into the original equation.\n- A second example is 3x - 5 = 10: add 5 to both sides to get 3x = 15.\n- Then divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5."
   }
